235
00:13:43,690 --> 00:13:45,310
You need to talk to someone about a project.

236
00:13:45,590 --> 00:13:46,270
You need a meeting.

237
00:13:46,290 --> 00:13:46,870
You're busy.

238
00:13:46,930 --> 00:13:47,480
They're busy.

239
00:13:47,820 --> 00:13:51,480
How do you start that conversation without coming across as totally demanding?

240
00:13:51,880 --> 00:13:57,210
All right, let's start with the phrase, our sources identify as the absolute worst one, the level one to direct translation.

241
00:13:57,450 --> 00:13:59,150
It is, we need to meet now.

242
00:13:59,730 --> 00:14:00,710
Oof.

243
00:14:00,730 --> 00:14:03,940
That feels like the linguistic equivalent of just kicking down their office door.

244
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:06,400
It is entirely too demanding.

245
00:14:06,420 --> 00:14:09,510
I mean, the source material highlights two main linguistic failures here.

246
00:14:09,550 --> 00:14:12,790
The first one is a temporal failure, the word now.

247
00:14:12,850 --> 00:14:21,320
When you use now, you just completely override the other person's existing time commitments, their priorities, their current mental flow.

248
00:14:21,780 --> 00:14:27,190
You are basically saying whatever you are doing is less important than what I need to talk about.

249
00:14:27,520 --> 00:14:36,510
That's an implied disrespect for their schedule, which in a professional context often gets interpreted as disrespect for their status, regardless of the actual hierarchy.

250
00:14:36,630 --> 00:14:37,210
Exactly.

251
00:14:37,370 --> 00:14:46,380
Even if you are their manager, using now for something that's not a true emergency, it just signals poor time management or a lack of planning on your part.

252
00:14:46,460 --> 00:14:47,200
That's a great point.

253
00:14:47,260 --> 00:14:56,430
In these highly structured work environments where schedules are visible and managed, demanding immediate unscheduled attention is a major social faux pas.

254
00:14:56,410 --> 00:14:57,780
Okay, so that's the first failure.

255
00:14:58,200 --> 00:14:59,560
The now, what's the second?

256
00:14:59,740 --> 00:15:02,030
The second failure is the phrase, we need to.

257
00:15:02,510 --> 00:15:08,350
That imperative structure, once again, sounds like a demand or language that's strictly reserved for an emergency.

258
00:15:08,370 --> 00:15:09,510
It's way too strong.

259
00:15:09,560 --> 00:15:11,020
It is far too strong.

260
00:15:11,120 --> 00:15:16,630
The sources highlight that this is the language of instruction that signals absolute necessity, a command.

261
00:15:17,390 --> 00:15:23,740
And if you use this upward to your management or even laterally to a colleague, you're just overstepping your relational bounds.

262
00:15:24,120 --> 00:15:27,800
You're dictating the terms of the interaction instead of soliciting collaboration.

263
00:15:28,020 --> 00:15:30,230
And the psychological response is probably defensiveness.

264
00:15:30,610 --> 00:15:31,070
Instantly.

265
00:15:31,270 --> 00:15:34,670
So let's pivot hard to level two, the basic improvement.

266
00:15:34,870 --> 00:15:37,180
We want to keep it simple, but make it professional.

267
00:15:37,960 --> 00:15:40,010
The fix here is to turn it into a question.

268
00:15:40,530 --> 00:15:41,780
Can we talk about the project?

269
00:15:42,040 --> 00:15:43,750
This is a foundational improvement.

270
00:15:43,850 --> 00:15:44,690
It's huge.

271
00:15:45,250 --> 00:15:50,390
Just by switching to the modal verb can and using question syntax, you introduce choice.

272
00:15:50,820 --> 00:15:52,120
You introduce deference.

273
00:15:52,440 --> 00:15:55,560
You're respecting their schedule by asking if they have time or if they're ready.

274
00:15:55,980 --> 00:15:57,440
It's just so much more considerate.

275
00:15:57,500 --> 00:15:57,820
It is.

276
00:15:57,840 --> 00:16:06,090
And it also immediately introduces the topic, the project, which gives essential context, something that level one phrase completely failed to do.

277
00:16:06,330 --> 00:16:15,480
And the sources gave that wonderful cultural note about American offices where people might do that sort of fake knock knock on the cubicle wall before asking this question.

278
00:16:15,500 --> 00:16:17,880
It's a physical signal of that polite buffer.

279
00:16:18,620 --> 00:16:21,410
I'm about to interrupt you, but I'm doing it gently.

280
00:16:21,790 --> 00:16:23,190
It illustrates the point perfectly.

281
00:16:23,590 --> 00:16:33,900
Even in spoken casual communication, the moment we interrupt someone, we instinctively use these social signals, whether they're physical or verbal, to soften the intrusion.

282
00:16:33,920 --> 00:16:44,120
But if we want to move from just basic politeness to level three, the clear and polite phrasing that really marks professional fluency, we make a crucial linguistic upgrade.

283
00:16:44,200 --> 00:16:49,030
This is the one you'll hear most often in casual yet respectful spoken requests.

284
00:16:49,830 --> 00:16:51,860
Could we set up a time to talk about the project?

285
00:16:51,840 --> 00:16:53,640
Now we are operating in the sweet spot.

286
00:16:53,660 --> 00:16:54,080
We are.

287
00:16:54,120 --> 00:16:59,470
The jump from can we to could we is technically an upgrade in formality.

288
00:16:59,970 --> 00:17:01,990
Could is the past tense of can.

289
00:17:02,350 --> 00:17:07,700
And using the past tense modal often creates a greater distance and therefore greater politeness in English requests.

290
00:17:07,900 --> 00:17:10,720
The real game changer here is that phrase set up a time.

291
00:17:10,820 --> 00:17:12,730
It's the direct antidote to now.

292
00:17:13,310 --> 00:17:14,010
Exactly.

293
00:17:14,050 --> 00:17:17,320
Set up a time removes the immediacy entirely.

294
00:17:17,360 --> 00:17:23,390
You are explicitly allowing the recipient to find a window in the future, respecting their existing commitments.

295
00:17:23,890 --> 00:17:25,250
This could be later this afternoon.

296
00:17:25,330 --> 00:17:26,190
It could be tomorrow.

297
00:17:26,270 --> 00:17:27,710
It could be next week.

298
00:17:27,730 --> 00:17:28,600
And crucially.

299
00:17:29,450 --> 00:17:31,720
The sources emphasize the psychological effect here.

300
00:17:32,220 --> 00:17:37,350
When you ask someone to schedule a meeting, you're engaging them as a partner in time management.

301
00:17:37,750 --> 00:17:39,950
You're not just treating them as an immediate resource.

302
00:17:40,210 --> 00:17:42,930
And that fosters much better professional relations.

303
00:17:43,070 --> 00:17:46,200
I can just imagine someone who uses level one language all the time.

304
00:17:47,320 --> 00:17:47,420
Yeah.

305
00:17:47,440 --> 00:17:50,690
They would develop a reputation for being frantic or disruptive.

306
00:17:50,870 --> 00:17:51,130
Yes.

307
00:17:51,530 --> 00:17:55,710
While someone consistently using level three is seen as organized and respectful.

308
00:17:55,930 --> 00:17:58,820
That is the professional currency we were talking about right at the start.

309
00:17:58,890 --> 00:18:00,080
And think about the structure.

310
00:18:00,320 --> 00:18:01,720
Could we set up a time to?

311
00:18:01,760 --> 00:18:06,570
That template is so reusable for almost any request for time or attention.

312
00:18:06,910 --> 00:18:07,190
Okay.

313
00:18:07,210 --> 00:18:12,940
And then there's level four, the former written version, perfect for an email request that needs to be tracked or documented.

314
00:18:13,180 --> 00:18:13,420
Right.

315
00:18:14,020 --> 00:18:17,900
Would it be possible to schedule a meeting to discuss the project in detail?

316
00:18:18,040 --> 00:18:20,400
This feels like the maximal politeness formulation.

317
00:18:20,820 --> 00:18:22,160
It's deliberately lengthy.

318
00:18:22,300 --> 00:18:23,010
It's formal.

319
00:18:23,290 --> 00:18:24,090
It's structured.

320
00:18:24,250 --> 00:18:24,550
Oh, yeah.

321
00:18:24,690 --> 00:18:27,170
For spoken English, it is definitely a mouthful.

322
00:18:27,830 --> 00:18:30,260
It might even sound a little awkward in a casual setting.

323
00:18:30,640 --> 00:18:32,700
But for writing, it's pitch perfect.

324
00:18:33,140 --> 00:18:37,110
The key is that entire formal chunk at the beginning.

325
00:18:37,090 --> 00:18:39,070
Would it be possible to schedule?

326
00:18:39,190 --> 00:18:40,310
Let's break that chunk down.

327
00:18:40,790 --> 00:18:45,940
The use of would instead of can or could adds another layer of hypothetical distance, right?

328
00:18:45,960 --> 00:18:47,540
It makes the request even less demanding.

329
00:18:47,660 --> 00:18:48,140
Absolutely.

330
00:18:48,740 --> 00:18:51,530
Would it be possible to schedule is a fixed phrase.

331
00:18:52,050 --> 00:18:55,370
It treats the meeting itself as a potential, not a necessity.

332
00:18:56,090 --> 00:18:59,520
And notice we also see the addition of in detail at the end.

333
00:18:59,500 --> 00:19:03,850
In formal written communication, we often provide slightly more context and detail.

334
00:19:04,370 --> 00:19:09,160
It ensures the recipient knows the scope of the discussion, which again is a sign of respect for their time.

335
00:19:09,200 --> 00:19:13,050
They can block out the right amount of time based on the detail you provide.

336
00:19:13,180 --> 00:19:20,890
So even though this is the longest phrase, it achieves maximum efficiency by being maximally polite and maximally clear about what you need.

337
00:19:21,230 --> 00:19:23,020
That's a powerful lesson in business English.

338
00:19:23,120 --> 00:19:23,740
It really is.

339
00:19:23,960 --> 00:19:30,370
Next up, a common request that carries, I think, a really high social risk if you handle it incorrectly.

340
00:19:31,110 --> 00:19:32,110
Requesting time off.

341
00:19:32,610 --> 00:19:33,390
Personal leave.

342
00:19:33,730 --> 00:19:34,010
Yes.

343
00:19:34,570 --> 00:19:42,800
This is when you need to be away from work, usually for highly personal reasons, and you have to communicate that need to your manager or your team.

344
00:19:42,780 --> 00:19:50,690
The level one phrase here, the too direct version that we often see when people rely on direct translation is, I'm not coming to work tomorrow or today.

345
00:19:50,950 --> 00:19:54,620
If that isn't an instantaneous conflict trigger, I don't know what is.

346
00:19:54,700 --> 00:19:57,500
It sounds less like a request and more like a declaration of war.

347
00:19:57,920 --> 00:19:59,840
That's exactly the feeling it conveys.

348
00:20:00,320 --> 00:20:06,710
The source material notes that this phrasing sounds like the speaker is being defiant or fighting back against some authority they resent.

349
00:20:07,390 --> 00:20:12,420
The vocabulary itself just conveys hostility, even if the speaker says it in a meek voice.

350
00:20:12,470 --> 00:20:15,440
The critical failure, though, seems to be what it implies about authority.

351
00:20:16,650 --> 00:20:18,860
You're stating the decision as a fait accompli.

352
00:20:19,240 --> 00:20:19,940
It's already done.

353
00:20:20,240 --> 00:20:21,750
You're failing to ask for permission.

354
00:20:21,850 --> 00:20:24,750
That is the essential failure in organizational English.

355
00:20:25,430 --> 00:20:31,880
Professionalism demands that you request authorization for time away from work, not simply announce your absence.

356
00:20:32,020 --> 00:20:32,480
Right.

357
00:20:32,500 --> 00:20:39,140
When you just state, I'm not coming in, it forces the manager to chase you down for details, assuming the decision wasn't cleared.

358
00:20:39,550 --> 00:20:42,370
It's perceived as just bypassing the established protocols.

359
00:20:42,770 --> 00:20:45,590
OK, so let's immediately move to level two, the basic improvement.

360
00:20:46,490 --> 00:20:48,950
We communicate the need, but we do it professionally.

361
00:20:49,150 --> 00:20:51,840
Which would be, I need a day off for personal reasons.

362
00:20:51,920 --> 00:20:54,480
This is a monumental shift toward professionalism.

363
00:20:54,740 --> 00:20:55,330
It really is.

364
00:20:55,790 --> 00:20:59,290
First, you're communicating the request clearly and, if possible, in advance.

365
00:20:59,950 --> 00:21:06,500
Second, that phrase, personal reasons, is a critical piece of business English vocabulary for privacy.

366
00:21:06,700 --> 00:21:07,900
It's the cultural shield, isn't it?

367
00:21:08,180 --> 00:21:08,700
Absolutely.

368
00:21:08,740 --> 00:21:16,230
In many English-speaking professional environments, there is a strong cultural boundary against sharing unnecessary medical or family details.

369
00:21:16,810 --> 00:21:21,100
Personal reasons is a perfectly acceptable, simple, and vague cover.

370
00:21:21,080 --> 00:21:25,650
And it communicates the seriousness of the need without requiring you to share sensitive information.

371
00:21:25,990 --> 00:21:26,250
Yes.

372
00:21:27,130 --> 00:21:32,140
It covers everything from a sick child to a doctor's appointment or even mental health time.

373
00:21:32,700 --> 00:21:36,300
It just signals this is necessary, but it's private.

374
00:21:36,480 --> 00:21:43,870
So it avoids that detailed explanation that could be seen as oversharing or on the flip side, as a fabricated excuse.

375
00:21:44,800 --> 00:21:46,500
It's efficient and it's private.

376
00:21:46,560 --> 00:21:47,220
Exactly.

377
00:21:47,260 --> 00:21:50,410
It conveys the request simply and professionally.

378
00:21:50,450 --> 00:21:54,970
However, even with level two, the verb I need is still a little bit demanding.

379
00:21:55,070 --> 00:21:57,000
It doesn't explicitly seek permission.

380
00:21:57,060 --> 00:21:57,580
No, it doesn't.

381
00:21:57,620 --> 00:22:01,860
Which is why we make that jump to level three, the clear and polite conversational phrasing.

382
00:22:02,220 --> 00:22:04,270
Could I take a day off for personal reasons?

383
00:22:04,290 --> 00:22:06,650
Now we are squarely in the polite request zone.

384
00:22:07,170 --> 00:22:12,180
We're using that key conditional phrase, could I, which signals that we're seeking authorization.

385
00:22:12,160 --> 00:22:15,750
And we're utilizing a linguistic pattern that manages professional expectations.

386
00:22:16,010 --> 00:22:21,500
As we learned in the first scenario, the consistent use of these modal verbs is just non-negotiable for professional politeness.

387
00:22:21,660 --> 00:22:25,040
I want to pause on the grammatical difference between can I and could I.

388
00:22:26,020 --> 00:22:27,570
When should a listener choose could?

389
00:22:28,210 --> 00:22:30,150
I think this is a really common point of confusion.

390
00:22:30,470 --> 00:22:31,510
That's a great question.

391
00:22:31,550 --> 00:22:37,220
While they're both requests, can I literally means am I able to.

392
00:22:38,030 --> 00:22:41,300
It's suitable for slightly more casual requests with colleagues.

393
00:22:41,920 --> 00:22:46,460
Could I often functions as the conditional and slightly more formal equivalent.

394
00:22:46,560 --> 00:22:50,490
It implies would it be possible for me to or would you allow me to.

395
00:22:51,770 --> 00:23:01,930
So in a request to management where deference is crucial could I is the superior choice because it subtly reinforces the manager's authority to grant or deny the request.

396
00:23:01,970 --> 00:23:06,200
So that slight linguistic shift reinforces the appropriate power dynamic.

397
00:23:06,320 --> 00:23:06,920
Precisely.

398
00:23:06,940 --> 00:23:13,840
Now, for the maximum politeness needed for level four, the formal written request, the structure you'd use for a formal HR email.

399
00:23:14,200 --> 00:23:17,880
I'd like to formally request a day off due to personal reasons, if that's all right with you.

400
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,730
OK, we see the use of highly formal vocabulary here.

401
00:23:21,190 --> 00:23:25,110
Formally request is clearly signaling a high stakes official action.

402
00:23:25,160 --> 00:23:29,670
Yes, and due to personal reasons is the written equivalent of the spoken for personal reasons.

403
00:23:29,850 --> 00:23:34,540
The phrase due to carries a greater weight of cause and effect, which is suitable for documentation.

404
00:23:34,880 --> 00:23:40,830
But the most important and often overlooked element is the addition of that politeness buffer at the end.

405
00:23:40,950 --> 00:23:48,160
Yes, if that's all right with you, or the slightly more casual but still acceptable, if that's okay with you.

406
00:23:48,350 --> 00:23:51,210
That phrase acts as the final safety net, doesn't it?

407
00:23:51,990 --> 00:23:57,940
It explicitly gives the manager the option to say no without the employee feeling completely undermined.

408
00:23:58,140 --> 00:24:00,310
It manages expectations brilliantly.

409
00:24:00,330 --> 00:24:04,640
It softens the request by acknowledging the possibility of denial.

410
00:24:04,820 --> 00:24:08,180
It shows that you understand the request might inconvenience the organization.

411
00:24:08,220 --> 00:24:13,730
The listener should practice adding this buffer to almost every request for time or resources.

412
00:24:13,710 --> 00:24:15,510
Like, could you send me the file if that's all right?

413
00:24:15,630 --> 00:24:16,130
Exactly.

414
00:24:16,170 --> 00:24:19,220
It just reduces the perceived pressure on the recipient.

415
00:24:19,540 --> 00:24:31,290
It truly emphasizes that the entire framework of professional language is about incorporating those maybe words and those conditional phrases to make sure you never sound like you're issuing a non-negotiable order.

416
00:24:31,470 --> 00:24:31,910
That's it.

417
00:24:32,590 --> 00:24:36,390
Let's move on to something that requires a high degree of vulnerability and sensitivity.

418
00:24:37,300 --> 00:24:40,840
Asking for feedback on your work performance or a finished project.

419
00:24:41,420 --> 00:24:42,620
Ooh, this is a tricky one.

420
00:24:42,740 --> 00:24:43,140
It is.

421
00:24:43,500 --> 00:24:49,680
You need constructive suggestions, but you have to approach the request in a way that suggests you are ready to receive them professionally.

422
00:24:50,200 --> 00:24:54,850
So if you are relying on a blunt translation, you might end up with a level one to direct disaster.

423
00:24:55,790 --> 00:24:57,470
Tell me if my work is good or bad.

424
00:24:58,450 --> 00:25:02,300
This phrasing just creates an immediate sense of anxiety and even aggression.

425
00:25:02,340 --> 00:25:05,060
The sources identify this as a double failure.

426
00:25:05,440 --> 00:25:07,180
The first is that imperative opening.

427
00:25:07,640 --> 00:25:08,000
Tell me.

428
00:25:08,250 --> 00:25:09,490
Again, that strong instruction.

429
00:25:09,890 --> 00:25:12,220
It's so inappropriate for peers or managers.

430
00:25:12,440 --> 00:25:12,960
Exactly.

431
00:25:12,980 --> 00:25:19,110
When you're seeking feedback, you have to adopt a stance of inquiry, not instruction.

432
00:25:19,610 --> 00:25:25,640
By using tell me, you are inadvertently asserting control over the conversation, demanding an answer immediately.

433
00:25:25,820 --> 00:25:27,760
You know, a teacher might say, tell me the answer, but.

434
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:28,870
I'm not a colleague.

435
00:25:28,880 --> 00:25:29,670
Not a colleague.

436
00:25:30,110 --> 00:25:34,710
Using this strong assertive language with a colleague or manager is professionally inappropriate.

437
00:25:34,690 --> 00:25:39,990
And the second, more nuanced failure, is the good or bad dichotomy.

438
00:25:40,010 --> 00:25:40,250
Yes.

439
00:25:40,670 --> 00:25:48,610
Native English speakers, especially in professional contexts, actively avoid this blunt binary structure when giving or asking for constructive feedback.

440
00:25:49,010 --> 00:25:49,250
Right.

441
00:25:49,330 --> 00:25:50,170
It's never that simple.

442
00:25:50,270 --> 00:25:51,250
It's never that simple.

443
00:25:51,370 --> 00:25:55,760
Professional feedback is almost never categorized as simply good or bad.

444
00:25:55,800 --> 00:26:00,840
It's about strengths, weaknesses, areas for improvement, next steps.

445
00:26:00,860 --> 00:26:08,890
If you ask for good or bad, you force the recipient into this awkward, overly simplistic and possibly confrontational position.

446
00:26:09,190 --> 00:26:12,300
You're basically asking them to judge you, not critique the work.

447
00:26:12,480 --> 00:26:12,900
That's it.

448
00:26:13,140 --> 00:26:14,460
Not a critique, a judgment.

449
00:26:14,500 --> 00:26:15,500
That makes so much sense.

450
00:26:15,880 --> 00:26:19,220
We want constructive language, not critical judgment.

451
00:26:19,240 --> 00:26:23,910
So we make the conversational shift with level two, the basic improvement.

452
00:26:23,930 --> 00:26:25,810
Which is, what do you think of my performance?

453
00:26:26,150 --> 00:26:27,370
This is a huge improvement.

454
00:26:27,890 --> 00:26:29,880
It opens the door without any confrontation.

455
00:26:30,360 --> 00:26:32,480
It's a simple, friendly question format.

456
00:26:32,960 --> 00:26:34,100
What do you think of the proposal?

457
00:26:34,540 --> 00:26:36,980
Or what do you think of the new marketing campaign?

458
00:26:37,130 --> 00:26:42,560
It transitions the interaction from an imperative command to an open invitation for a dialogue.

459
00:26:42,640 --> 00:26:44,640
It's polite enough for a quick verbal check-in.

460
00:26:45,050 --> 00:26:50,070
So moving to level three, the polished conversational phrase, clear and polite.

461
00:26:50,590 --> 00:26:51,120
That would be.

462
00:26:51,190 --> 00:26:52,720
Can I get your thoughts on my performance so far?

463
00:26:53,060 --> 00:26:56,400
We rely on that reliable, polite request structure again.

464
00:26:56,520 --> 00:26:57,080
Can I get?

465
00:26:57,100 --> 00:26:57,530
We do.

466
00:26:57,570 --> 00:27:01,770
This formulation is universally accepted and it's non-confrontational.

467
00:27:02,170 --> 00:27:07,870
It frames the feedback request as seeking a resource, their thoughts, rather than demanding a judgment.

468
00:27:08,450 --> 00:27:14,900
This phrasing implies that the speaker is prepared to accept various types of feedback, whether it's positive, negative or complex.

469
00:27:14,920 --> 00:27:20,450
I also think the addition of so far is really crucial in this conversational setting.

470
00:27:20,470 --> 00:27:22,490
It is subtle but vital.

471
00:27:22,730 --> 00:27:30,150
So far signals that the work may not be totally final and that you're looking for input on the current state before you do a full submission.

472
00:27:31,020 --> 00:27:33,660
It communicates humility and a collaborative spirit.

473
00:27:33,860 --> 00:27:34,720
That's a good distinction.

474
00:27:34,840 --> 00:27:38,040
If you just ask her thoughts on my performance, it sounds so definitive.

475
00:27:38,240 --> 00:27:43,910
But if you ask about performance so far, you're inviting the manager into the process before it's too late to make changes.

476
00:27:44,210 --> 00:27:45,850
OK, here's where it gets really interesting, though.

477
00:27:46,470 --> 00:27:52,300
Level four, the formal written request, maybe for an annual review or a formal project debrief.

478
00:27:52,280 --> 00:27:56,220
That would be, could I get your feedback on my recent performance when it's convenient for you?

479
00:27:56,600 --> 00:28:02,650
This utilizes the upgraded formality of could I get over can I get, which ensures maximum deference.

480
00:28:03,470 --> 00:28:07,240
But the essential non-negotiable addition here is that time buffer.

481
00:28:07,880 --> 00:28:09,140
When it's convenient for you.

482
00:28:09,300 --> 00:28:16,710
We've seen this time buffer repeatedly now, but why is it so powerful in a request for feedback specifically?

483
00:28:16,850 --> 00:28:20,070
Because feedback requires mental effort and dedicated time.

484
00:28:20,510 --> 00:28:22,190
It's not a quick yes or no answer.

485
00:28:22,390 --> 00:28:22,610
Right.

486
00:28:22,930 --> 00:28:32,990
By adding, when it's congenient for you, you are acknowledging that giving thoughtful feedback requires them to switch gears, maybe review documents, and actually write out their thoughts.

487
00:28:33,410 --> 00:28:36,870
You're signaling that you respect the time commitment required to assist you.

488
00:28:36,890 --> 00:28:38,680
It eliminates the implicit pressure.

489
00:28:38,780 --> 00:28:43,500
If I get an email that just says, can I get your feedback, I feel pressure to drop everything and reply.

490
00:28:43,860 --> 00:28:45,380
But when it's convenient,

491
00:28:45,550 --> 00:28:47,510
It instantly lowers my cognitive load.

492
00:28:47,530 --> 00:28:48,210
I can schedule it.

493
00:28:48,660 --> 00:28:49,180
Absolutely.

494
00:28:49,480 --> 00:28:57,370
And the source material notes that this phrase actually makes people more open to helping you, paradoxically, because you have respected their autonomy over their own schedule.

495
00:28:58,230 --> 00:29:01,360
This is the currency of professional politeness in action.

496
00:29:02,020 --> 00:29:04,870
Showing deference for time generates immediate goodwill.

497
00:29:05,350 --> 00:29:07,590
Okay, let's look at the flip side of collaboration.

498
00:29:08,320 --> 00:29:09,500
Giving an update on your work.

499
00:29:09,520 --> 00:29:16,410
You've finished your section or you've reached a milestone and now you need to hand it off or at least inform the team of your progress.

500
00:29:17,090 --> 00:29:22,710
The level one to direct version of this is painfully simple and often frustrating for the person receiving it.

501
00:29:22,970 --> 00:29:23,580
Here's my work.

502
00:29:23,760 --> 00:29:24,860
I can almost picture the scene.

503
00:29:25,460 --> 00:29:30,420
Someone walking over, dropping a massive binder on the desk and just walking away.

504
00:29:30,620 --> 00:29:31,980
That's the feeling it conveys.

505
00:29:32,170 --> 00:29:35,150
It gives zero context and zero additional information.

506
00:29:35,190 --> 00:29:40,120
The source material explains that this phrase sounds like the work is being dumped.

507
00:29:40,680 --> 00:29:44,810
The implicit message is, I'm done, and this is now your administrative burden.

508
00:29:45,450 --> 00:29:50,120
You're signaling finality and shutting the door on any further conversation or necessary collaboration.

509
00:29:50,210 --> 00:29:52,860
It also fails to clarify what they're supposed to do with it.

510
00:29:53,640 --> 00:29:54,500
Is it for review?

511
00:29:55,000 --> 00:29:56,200
Is it for implementation?

512
00:29:56,260 --> 00:29:57,680
Is it just for their files?

513
00:29:57,980 --> 00:29:58,450
Exactly.

514
00:29:58,870 --> 00:30:04,510
It forces the recipient to ask all the follow-up questions, which makes your communication less efficient overall.

515
00:30:04,730 --> 00:30:09,120
You want to communicate continuity and progress, not just abrupt finality.

516
00:30:09,160 --> 00:30:12,720
That brings us to level two, the basic improvement.

517
00:30:13,320 --> 00:30:16,090
We introduced the crucial element of context.

518
00:30:16,170 --> 00:30:20,340
Which would be, here's an update on the project, or here's what I've done so far.

519
00:30:20,390 --> 00:30:25,920
The words update and so far are central to establishing that collaborative context.

520
00:30:25,940 --> 00:30:26,360
They are.

521
00:30:26,620 --> 00:30:29,050
Update communicates that this is a continuing process.

522
00:30:29,130 --> 00:30:30,890
It's part of a larger moving target.

523
00:30:31,290 --> 00:30:34,500
It keeps the conversation open for continuation or necessary fixes.

524
00:30:35,100 --> 00:30:41,050
So far indicates that this is the progress up to this moment, suggesting the work will proceed if more is required.

525
00:30:41,030 --> 00:30:45,510
Whereas if you just say, here's my work, it implies the speaker has just washed their hands of the task.

526
00:30:45,690 --> 00:30:46,430
Yes.

527
00:30:46,450 --> 00:30:50,320
That structural change makes a massive difference in how the information is received.

528
00:30:50,940 --> 00:30:55,070
Now for the polished conversational choice, level three, clear and polite.

529
00:30:55,450 --> 00:30:58,280
I just wanted to update you on my progress with the project.

530
00:30:58,470 --> 00:31:04,550
This is an exemplary piece of conversational business English, and it functions primarily as a sophisticated time buffer.

531
00:31:04,570 --> 00:31:10,860
The opening, I just wanted to update you, immediately signals respect for the recipient's time.

532
00:31:10,960 --> 00:31:11,440
How so?

533
00:31:11,760 --> 00:31:15,060
It implies a quick, non-disruptive interaction.

534
00:31:15,120 --> 00:31:20,090
You are pre-framing the communication as concise, saying, I know you are busy.

535
00:31:20,250 --> 00:31:23,860
I am only taking a moment of your time to provide a necessary status check.

536
00:31:24,140 --> 00:31:28,310
It's the conversational equivalent of knocking and then stepping back slightly.

537
00:31:28,450 --> 00:31:28,970
Precisely.

538
00:31:29,390 --> 00:31:37,390
And the vocabulary choice of progress reinforces that the work is ongoing and the speaker is open to collaborative solutions or feedback.

539
00:31:38,010 --> 00:31:40,800
It emphasizes movement rather than stasis.

540
00:31:40,900 --> 00:31:46,150
But wait, in this source material, why do they emphasize I just wanted to rather than just I wanted to?

541
00:31:46,170 --> 00:31:48,130
Is that little word just actually important?

542
00:31:48,350 --> 00:31:50,890
That's a subtle but fascinating linguistic detail.

543
00:31:51,010 --> 00:31:55,660
The word just here functions as an intensifier that actually minimizes the scope of the action.

544
00:31:55,740 --> 00:31:56,400
Oh, that's interesting.

545
00:31:56,740 --> 00:32:00,570
If I say I wanted to update you, it sounds deliberate and formal.

546
00:32:01,090 --> 00:32:07,600
If I say I just wanted to update you, it suggests the action is quick, small, and almost apologetic for the intrusion.

547
00:32:08,080 --> 00:32:11,690
It further maximizes the deference to the recipient's schedule.

548
00:32:11,810 --> 00:32:15,330
It's a very common, almost invisible politeness marker.

549
00:32:15,490 --> 00:32:16,610
That's truly insightful.

550
00:32:17,150 --> 00:32:20,780
And finally, level four, the highly formal written statement.

551
00:32:20,800 --> 00:32:24,020
Perfect for an email closing out a phase or a formal report.

552
00:32:24,640 --> 00:32:28,490
I'd like to provide you with an update on the progress I've made on the project thus far.

553
00:32:28,540 --> 00:32:30,500
We shift to very formal vocabulary here.

554
00:32:30,920 --> 00:32:35,050
I noticed the verb choice, provide, instead of give or hand over.

555
00:32:35,090 --> 00:32:38,840
Yes, provide is an elevated term suitable for documentation.

556
00:32:39,540 --> 00:32:43,380
We also see nominalization in the phrase, the progress I've met on the project.

557
00:32:43,590 --> 00:32:47,990
And let's dive into that final, very written vocabulary choice, thus far.

558
00:32:48,230 --> 00:32:52,080
Thus far is what the sources identify as a classic written formality.

559
00:32:52,450 --> 00:33:00,080
It means exactly the same thing as so far up to this point, but it carries significantly more weight and professionalism in written communication.

560
00:33:00,600 --> 00:33:03,370
So you would almost never use thus far in a casual conversation.

561
00:33:03,650 --> 00:33:04,330
Almost never.

562
00:33:04,710 --> 00:33:06,090
It sounds overly stiff.

563
00:33:06,650 --> 00:33:11,500
However, in a professional email or report, it elevates the entire tone.

564
00:33:11,840 --> 00:33:14,830
It signals precision and official documentation.

565
00:33:15,130 --> 00:33:24,760
So if a listener wants to sound professionally fluent in writing, they should integrate thus far into their formal updates, but reserve so far for spoken dialogue.

566
00:33:25,160 --> 00:33:27,120
It's all about matching the register to the medium.

567
00:33:27,380 --> 00:33:27,960
Exactly.

568
00:33:28,300 --> 00:33:35,490
The final scenario is one where direct translation can lead to immediate conflict because it involves admitting a lack of understanding.

569
00:33:35,550 --> 00:33:36,550
Asking for clarification.

570
00:33:36,810 --> 00:33:37,070
Yes.

571
00:33:37,430 --> 00:33:43,920
Asking for clarification in a meeting when you don't understand instructions or a technical phrase or some complex process.

572
00:33:43,900 --> 00:33:47,900
If you rely on the simplest translation, you get the level one to direct options.

573
00:33:47,960 --> 00:33:49,470
What are you talking about or what are you saying?

574
00:33:50,630 --> 00:33:55,690
These phrases are fundamentally hostile and the sources label them as immediately accusatory.

575
00:33:55,710 --> 00:34:03,080
Even though the speaker genuinely just needs clarity, the structure of the question sounds like intense disbelief or confrontation.

576
00:34:03,120 --> 00:34:06,090
It implies your speech is incoherent.

577
00:34:06,210 --> 00:34:07,850
Therefore, I cannot understand you.

578
00:34:08,050 --> 00:34:12,280
It squarely places the fault, the lack of understanding on the other person.

579
00:34:12,940 --> 00:34:14,580
You were talking crazy, so I'm confused.

580
00:34:14,660 --> 00:34:19,430
It's aggressive, it's accusatory, and it's completely inappropriate in a professional context.

581
00:34:19,670 --> 00:34:28,980
Which brings us to perhaps the single most important politeness technique in business English, shifting the perspective to avoid assigning blame.

582
00:34:29,020 --> 00:34:32,470
Which is perfectly executed in level two, the basic improvement.

583
00:34:33,560 --> 00:34:35,570
I don't quite understand the task.

584
00:34:35,710 --> 00:34:37,890
This is essential for professional survival.

585
00:34:38,190 --> 00:34:44,620
In office English, especially when dealing with correction or lack of clarity, we must avoid the accusatory you.

586
00:34:44,660 --> 00:34:48,370
We focus the sentence on I, we, or the problem itself.

587
00:34:48,570 --> 00:34:52,760
So by saying I don't understand, you take the responsibility for the information gap.

588
00:34:53,160 --> 00:34:57,220
You're saying the problem is with my comprehension, not your ability to explain.

589
00:34:57,200 --> 00:34:57,850
You've got it.

590
00:34:58,210 --> 00:35:02,290
And that tiny linguistic buffer word, quite, plays a huge role here.

591
00:35:02,330 --> 00:35:02,910
How so?

592
00:35:02,930 --> 00:35:05,380
It's a masterful piece of linguistic finesse.

593
00:35:05,910 --> 00:35:10,380
The word quite subtly suggests that you understand most of it, maybe 80% or 90%.

594
00:35:11,220 --> 00:35:13,580
But you just need clarification on the remainder.

595
00:35:14,280 --> 00:35:21,290
It shows you were listening actively and that your request for a re-explanation is specific, not a blanket admission of total failure.

596
00:35:21,850 --> 00:35:26,420
So it makes the clarification request far less demanding and more acceptable to the speaker.

597
00:35:26,650 --> 00:35:31,840
It validates their effort, which is essential for maintaining positive professional relationships.

598
00:35:32,320 --> 00:35:39,710
That distinction from I understand nothing to I don't quite understand is a huge psychological shift for the recipient.

599
00:35:39,830 --> 00:35:40,110
It is.

600
00:35:40,630 --> 00:35:47,300
So we take that understanding and move to the level three clear and polite phrasing, which is the most common spoken request.

601
00:35:47,640 --> 00:35:49,220
Can you explain the task for me again?

602
00:35:49,440 --> 00:35:53,400
We're using you again here, but now it's buffered by the conditional can you.

603
00:35:53,430 --> 00:35:53,890
That's right.

604
00:35:53,990 --> 00:36:00,640
The polite question structure, can you, makes the use of you perfectly acceptable and entirely common in spoken English.

605
00:36:01,140 --> 00:36:05,570
It's clear, respectful, and it achieves the goal of seeking clarity without hostility.

606
00:36:06,070 --> 00:36:09,910
This is your go-to phrase if you need a quick restatement of instructions in a meeting.

607
00:36:10,270 --> 00:36:15,440
But I wonder, if the task is highly technical, should we use a different word than explain?

608
00:36:15,670 --> 00:36:17,840
That is precisely what Level 4 addresses.

609
00:36:17,960 --> 00:36:18,180
Yeah.

610
00:36:18,360 --> 00:36:22,780
For a complex or critical request via email, we have the formal written version.

611
00:36:23,190 --> 00:36:25,830
Could you clarify the details of the task for me, please?

612
00:36:25,850 --> 00:36:29,680
The formality upgrade from can you to could you is expected now.

613
00:36:29,800 --> 00:36:30,040
Yes.

614
00:36:30,620 --> 00:36:33,540
But the key distinction for written language is the verb choice.

615
00:36:34,260 --> 00:36:34,970
Clarify.

616
00:36:35,450 --> 00:36:45,600
While explain is perfectly common and polite in spoken conversation, the word clarify, which means to make clear, is a staple of professional written communication.

617
00:36:45,960 --> 00:36:48,050
You'll see it in emails and reports all the time.

618
00:36:48,290 --> 00:36:52,450
Why do you think clarify feels more professional than explain in written documents?

619
00:36:52,470 --> 00:36:53,290
That's a great question.

620
00:36:53,350 --> 00:36:58,620
Clarify often feels more precise and less remedial than explain.

621
00:36:58,740 --> 00:36:59,220
Remedial.

622
00:36:59,320 --> 00:37:00,220
Yeah.

623
00:37:00,240 --> 00:37:04,890
When you ask someone to explain, it can imply that the initial explanation was confusing or faulty.

624
00:37:05,370 --> 00:37:14,580
When you ask them to clarify, you are requesting precision or elaboration on a detail, which aligns perfectly with the need for accurate documentation and technical detail in the workplace.

625
00:37:14,860 --> 00:37:19,670
So it shifts the focus from the act of explaining to the quality of the details themselves.

626
00:37:19,690 --> 00:37:20,210
Exactly.

627
00:37:20,470 --> 00:37:25,570
I would guess that clarify appears far more often than explain in any professional's inbox.

628
00:37:25,590 --> 00:37:34,380
It's fascinating how the medium spoken versus written dictates the subtle shift in optimal vocabulary, even when the underlying meaning is the same.

629
00:37:34,360 --> 00:37:39,130
It reinforces that mastery is about having the right tool for the right job.

630
00:37:39,330 --> 00:37:39,990
Absolutely.

631
00:37:40,110 --> 00:37:43,090
We spent a lot of time breaking down five high stakes scenarios.

632
00:37:43,690 --> 00:37:51,060
And I think the wealth of examples allows us to pull out three crucial, universally applicable strategies for professional communication.

633
00:37:51,420 --> 00:37:56,860
These are the synthesis points, the big picture knowledge that will take your business English confidence to the next level.

634
00:37:57,340 --> 00:38:04,270
The first, the most structurally essential technique we saw applied across all five scenarios is the power of conditional language.

635
00:38:04,400 --> 00:38:04,740
Yes.

636
00:38:05,300 --> 00:38:15,580
Every single polite request, whether for a meeting, time off, or feedback use modal verbs, we saw can, could, or that fixed extended phrase, would it be possible to schedule.

637
00:38:16,020 --> 00:38:25,310
The moment you deploy a modal verb, you're signaling that you are making a request conditional on the recipient's capacity, not just issuing a command based on your necessity.

638
00:38:25,690 --> 00:38:30,120
It softens the entire interaction, making it inherently respectful.

639
00:38:30,370 --> 00:38:31,450
This is not optional.

640
00:38:32,130 --> 00:38:40,710
The shift from I need this to could I request this is the simplest, most fundamental professional upgrade a listener can make.

641
00:38:40,730 --> 00:38:44,530
And linguistically, these conditional forms create psychological safety.

642
00:38:44,610 --> 00:38:44,990
They do.

643
00:38:45,010 --> 00:38:51,260
They introduce space for the recipient to consider their resources without feeling immediately obligated or defensive.

644
00:38:51,580 --> 00:38:57,910
They communicate, I recognize your autonomy and I'm asking if you are willing and able to comply.

645
00:38:58,400 --> 00:39:00,980
This is the foundation of courteous professional interaction.

646
00:39:01,300 --> 00:39:07,850
And this leads directly to the second universal strategy, the necessity of respecting time through deliberate linguistic buffers.

647
00:39:08,150 --> 00:39:10,450
Because time is the ultimate professional currency.

648
00:39:10,510 --> 00:39:15,980
We saw phrases like, when it's convenient for you, if that's all right with you, and that quick pre-frame, I just wanted to update you.

649
00:39:16,100 --> 00:39:17,700
These are not just polite filler words.

650
00:39:18,020 --> 00:39:18,460
Not at all.

651
00:39:18,680 --> 00:39:24,390
They are essential structural components that manage the recipient's expectations about urgency and time commitment.

652
00:39:24,560 --> 00:39:27,380
I think we need to reiterate the cultural significance here.

653
00:39:27,700 --> 00:39:28,160
Yeah.

654
00:39:28,180 --> 00:39:35,130
Why is acknowledging the other person's busy schedule so crucial in, say, a Western professional context?

655
00:39:35,470 --> 00:39:39,260
It reflects a high value placed on individual scheduling and efficiency.

656
00:39:39,940 --> 00:39:45,730
By adding these buffers, you demonstrate that you view the recipient's time as a scarce and valuable resource.

657
00:39:46,310 --> 00:39:49,670
You are telling them, I am not demanding you drop everything for my need.

658
00:39:49,890 --> 00:39:52,440
And that small act of deference reduces friction.

659
00:39:52,860 --> 00:39:54,260
It creates immense goodwill.

660
00:39:54,320 --> 00:39:57,870
And it promotes a collaborative environment where people feel comfortable assisting you.

661
00:39:58,290 --> 00:40:02,970
If you skip this, you are effectively suggesting your priorities supersede theirs without warrant.

662
00:40:03,140 --> 00:40:04,040
That makes perfect sense.

663
00:40:04,840 --> 00:40:12,990
And finally, the third strategy, which was paramount in the clarification scenario but applies any time correction or disagreement is necessary, is avoiding blame.

664
00:40:13,710 --> 00:40:16,410
Or mastering the I versus you rule.

665
00:40:16,490 --> 00:40:20,180
This is a critical conversational pattern for avoiding conflict.

666
00:40:20,730 --> 00:40:37,560
In situations requiring clarification, correction, or when delivering bad news, phrasing the sentence to focus on the speaker, I don't quite understand, or I see some gaps in my understanding, rather than what are you talking about, or your plan is wrong, avoid sounding accusatory.

667
00:40:38,020 --> 00:40:46,590
It manages the potential for hostility by shifting the issue from a personal fault to a shared operational problem, or even just the speaker's personal challenge.

668
00:40:46,740 --> 00:40:52,380
By focusing on I or we, you depersonalize the potential confrontation.

669
00:40:52,840 --> 00:40:56,490
Instead of attacking the recipient's competence, you focus on the solution.

670
00:40:56,630 --> 00:40:59,060
I need help or let's work together on this.

671
00:40:59,580 --> 00:41:04,710
This dramatically lowers the potential for defensiveness and ensures the conversation stays constructive.

672
00:41:04,890 --> 00:41:07,140
Which is the goal of all professional communication.

673
00:41:07,160 --> 00:41:07,860
That's the goal.

674
00:41:07,980 --> 00:41:13,430
These three techniques, conditional language, temporal buffers, and perspective shifting, are your core set of tools.

675
00:41:13,830 --> 00:41:15,730
They don't just change the grammar of your sentences.

676
00:41:16,050 --> 00:41:19,040
They fundamentally change the professional reputation you project.

677
00:41:19,200 --> 00:41:22,790
If you internalize these patterns, you won't just be speaking clear English.

678
00:41:23,270 --> 00:41:31,280
You will be speaking professionally fluent English in a way that is immediately understood and respected by native speakers in any work environment.

679
00:41:31,740 --> 00:41:33,880
What an incredibly detailed deep dive.

680
00:41:34,480 --> 00:41:47,890
We've moved from those blunt, direct translations that can inadvertently sabotage your professional impression to a full toolkit of polished, polite phrases that ensure your communication is not only understood, well-received.

681
00:41:47,990 --> 00:41:52,420
And if you have used the two direct versions before, please don't worry about past interactions.

682
00:41:52,520 --> 00:41:54,640
People still understood your meaning.

683
00:41:55,100 --> 00:41:57,490
But now you have the better, more polished tools.

684
00:41:57,640 --> 00:42:04,030
You have the knowledge that ensures your point is delivered respectfully and thoughtfully, maximizing your chances of a successful outcome.

685
00:42:04,570 --> 00:42:09,360
This is about taking your professional English to the next level of confident, nuanced communication.

686
00:42:09,530 --> 00:42:10,650
It truly is transformative.

687
00:42:10,670 --> 00:42:21,810
And when you think about it, these nuances in language, the conscious choice of could I over I need, or the strategic deployment of if that's all right with you, aren't just about good manners.

688
00:42:22,230 --> 00:42:24,010
No, they are organizational currency.

689
00:42:24,390 --> 00:42:25,070
They really are.

690
00:42:25,440 --> 00:42:26,740
Think about this as we wrap up.

691
00:42:27,700 --> 00:42:39,040
How does the consistent conscious adoption of these politeness buffers fundamentally alter the perceived hierarchy and foster better professional relationships within your organization?

692
00:42:39,740 --> 00:42:47,410
Is your language framing your colleague as an obedient resource to be instructed or as an esteemed partner to be collaborated with?

693
00:42:47,550 --> 00:42:50,970
The language you choose dictates the nature of that professional relationship.

694
00:42:51,290 --> 00:42:55,220
It's something powerful to mull over as you start applying this knowledge next week.

695
00:42:55,600 --> 00:42:58,810
Thank you for joining us for this deep dive into mastering professional politeness.

696
00:42:59,070 --> 00:43:04,920
We hope these phrases help you feel more confident and successful during every discussion with your boss and your coworkers.

